    # brought across a rebase is stale the moment a sibling branch adds or removes a
    # record. Measured three times on three trees inside one day: 102, then 110, then
    # 111. Twice the arithmetic happened to agree, which is worse than it failing —
    # an agreeing subtraction reads as a derivation. `parked-drain`'s figures are the
    # counter-example that settles it: carrying blocks went 13 → 15 while the
    # population went 102 → 103, so the unchecked count FELL, 89 → 88. A subtraction
    # whose inputs both move is how a stale figure keeps looking current.
    print("       RE-MEASURE, never carry: this constant describes ONE tree, so a",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       value brought across a rebase is stale as soon as a sibling",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       branch adds or removes a record. Do not compute it either - an",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       agreeing subtraction reads as a derivation and is a coincidence.",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print(file=sys.stderr)
    # And the sentence that matters most, because of where the wrong remedy pointed.
    #
    # This failure used to be reported by the anchor-rot summary below, whose remedy
    # block discusses DELETING A REGISTRY ROW — the one action that turns a check into
    # a silent pass. So a stale count was answered with a procedure for removing a
    # guard. The counter split stops that summary firing here; this line stops the
    # reading surviving in a contributor's head.
    print("       NOTHING here asks you to delete a row. A stale count is a stale",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       count: no anchor moved, no record is unreplayable, and removing",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       an entry would turn a working check into a silent pass.",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print(file=sys.stderr)

